1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,306
Sweetheart, American goulash is the supper that

2
00:00:03,306 --> 00:00:05,908
fed half the families in this country

3
00:00:05,908 --> 00:00:07,174
for seventy years.

4
00:00:07,354 --> 00:00:09,735
Elbow macaroni, ground beef,

5
00:00:09,735 --> 00:00:12,201
and tomatoes, all in one pot,

6
00:00:12,201 --> 00:00:15,092
rich and saucy and ready in thirty

7
00:00:15,092 --> 00:00:15,772
minutes.

8
00:00:15,952 --> 00:00:18,352
But most of the goulash I taste

9
00:00:18,352 --> 00:00:19,901
is watery and bland,

10
00:00:19,901 --> 00:00:22,767
or the macaroni is swollen and mushy.

11
00:00:22,947 --> 00:00:26,221
And it's always the same five mistakes.

12
00:00:26,401 --> 00:00:28,607
Every one is easy to fix.

13
00:00:28,957 --> 00:00:31,433
So tonight I'm giving you the goulash

14
00:00:31,433 --> 00:00:33,843
my mother made every Thursday on Elm

15
00:00:33,843 --> 00:00:35,249
Street, step by step.

16
00:00:35,429 --> 00:00:36,631
And stay with me,

17
00:00:36,631 --> 00:00:39,247
because the last secret is one little

18
00:00:39,247 --> 00:00:41,862
thing from the spice cabinet that she

19
00:00:41,862 --> 00:00:43,771
stirred in at the very end,

20
00:00:43,771 --> 00:00:46,387
and it's why the neighbor kids always

21
00:00:46,387 --> 00:00:48,437
seemed to show up at our back

22
00:00:48,437 --> 00:00:49,709
door on Thursdays.

23
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:53,299
First, honey, I have to confess something.

24
00:00:53,479 --> 00:00:57,635
American goulash isn't really goulash at all.

25
00:00:57,815 --> 00:01:00,648
Real goulash comes from Hungary.

26
00:01:00,828 --> 00:01:03,986
It's a slow-cooked beef stew with lots

27
00:01:03,986 --> 00:01:06,563
of paprika and no pasta at all.

28
00:01:06,743 --> 00:01:10,130
Hungarian cowherds made it in big kettles

29
00:01:10,130 --> 00:01:11,451
over open fires,

30
00:01:11,451 --> 00:01:13,268
hundreds of years ago.

31
00:01:13,448 --> 00:01:16,478
The word gulyás even means herdsman.

32
00:01:16,658 --> 00:01:18,820
In a minute I'll tell you how

33
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:21,430
it turned into macaroni in America.

34
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:24,746
When Hungarian families came to America,

35
00:01:24,746 --> 00:01:26,377
they brought the name.

36
00:01:26,557 --> 00:01:28,277
And American home cooks,

37
00:01:28,277 --> 00:01:30,641
especially during the Depression,

38
00:01:30,641 --> 00:01:32,648
took that name and put it on

39
00:01:32,648 --> 00:01:35,442
whatever they could make cheaply in one

40
00:01:35,442 --> 00:01:35,729
pot.

41
00:01:35,909 --> 00:01:37,848
Macaroni was cheap.

42
00:01:38,028 --> 00:01:40,129
Canned tomatoes were cheap.

43
00:01:40,309 --> 00:01:43,105
A pound of ground beef could feed

44
00:01:43,105 --> 00:01:43,444
six.

45
00:01:43,624 --> 00:01:45,429
By the nineteen fifties,

46
00:01:45,429 --> 00:01:48,513
goulash was in every school cafeteria and

47
00:01:48,513 --> 00:01:50,845
church cookbook in the Midwest.

48
00:01:51,025 --> 00:01:53,364
Some people call it chili mac or

49
00:01:53,364 --> 00:01:54,241
slumgullion.

50
00:01:54,421 --> 00:01:55,753
In our house,

51
00:01:55,753 --> 00:01:57,289
it was goulash.

52
00:01:57,639 --> 00:02:00,506
Thursday was the night before payday,

53
00:02:00,506 --> 00:02:00,971
honey.

54
00:02:01,151 --> 00:02:04,564
The icebox was nearly empty by Thursday.

55
00:02:04,744 --> 00:02:06,776
So my mother made goulash,

56
00:02:06,776 --> 00:02:09,200
because all it took was a pound

57
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:11,311
of beef, a box of macaroni,

58
00:02:11,311 --> 00:02:13,578
and a couple of cans from the

59
00:02:13,578 --> 00:02:14,125
pantry.

60
00:02:14,305 --> 00:02:16,454
And somehow, every Thursday,

61
00:02:16,454 --> 00:02:19,217
there were one or two extra children

62
00:02:19,217 --> 00:02:20,214
at our table.

63
00:02:20,394 --> 00:02:23,320
The Kowalski boys from next door.

64
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:25,578
My mother never said a word.

65
00:02:25,758 --> 00:02:28,730
She just added another cup of macaroni.

66
00:02:29,080 --> 00:02:31,288
Those boys were seven and nine,

67
00:02:31,288 --> 00:02:34,136
honey, and their father worked nights at

68
00:02:34,136 --> 00:02:34,990
the foundry.

69
00:02:35,170 --> 00:02:37,933
Their mother cleaned houses during the day.

70
00:02:38,113 --> 00:02:39,343
So on Thursdays,

71
00:02:39,343 --> 00:02:42,725
by suppertime, there was usually nobody home

72
00:02:42,725 --> 00:02:45,416
and nothing in their icebox either.

73
00:02:45,596 --> 00:02:47,506
The first time they came,

74
00:02:47,506 --> 00:02:50,179
they just stood at the screen door,

75
00:02:50,179 --> 00:02:50,867
sniffing.

76
00:02:51,047 --> 00:02:53,183
My mother opened it and said,

77
00:02:53,183 --> 00:02:55,319
well, don't let the flies in.

78
00:02:55,499 --> 00:02:57,357
Come wash your hands.

79
00:02:57,537 --> 00:02:59,530
I'll tell you how she got paid

80
00:02:59,530 --> 00:03:00,660
back in a minute.

81
00:03:01,009 --> 00:03:03,749
Here's what you need for six people.

82
00:03:03,929 --> 00:03:06,399
One and a half pounds of ground

83
00:03:06,399 --> 00:03:06,797
beef.

84
00:03:06,977 --> 00:03:08,263
One big onion,

85
00:03:08,263 --> 00:03:08,997
chopped.

86
00:03:09,177 --> 00:03:11,152
One green bell pepper,

87
00:03:11,152 --> 00:03:11,871
chopped.

88
00:03:12,051 --> 00:03:13,955
Three cloves of garlic.

89
00:03:14,135 --> 00:03:16,343
One can of crushed tomatoes,

90
00:03:16,343 --> 00:03:17,920
twenty-eight ounces.

91
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:20,357
One can of tomato sauce,

92
00:03:20,357 --> 00:03:21,768
fifteen ounces.

93
00:03:21,948 --> 00:03:24,433
Two tablespoons of tomato paste.

94
00:03:24,613 --> 00:03:26,598
Two cups of beef broth.

95
00:03:26,778 --> 00:03:29,808
And two cups of dry elbow macaroni.

96
00:03:30,158 --> 00:03:33,310
For seasoning, a tablespoon of paprika,

97
00:03:33,310 --> 00:03:35,895
a teaspoon of Italian seasoning,

98
00:03:35,895 --> 00:03:38,885
a tablespoon of Worcestershire sauce,

99
00:03:38,885 --> 00:03:40,097
two bay leaves,

100
00:03:40,097 --> 00:03:41,632
a teaspoon of salt,

101
00:03:41,632 --> 00:03:44,056
and half a teaspoon of pepper.

102
00:03:44,236 --> 00:03:46,619
And a cup of shredded sharp cheddar

103
00:03:46,619 --> 00:03:47,436
for the top,

104
00:03:47,436 --> 00:03:48,253
if you like.

105
00:03:48,433 --> 00:03:50,540
Plus the secret ingredient,

106
00:03:50,540 --> 00:03:52,961
which I'll tell you at the end.

107
00:03:53,141 --> 00:03:55,985
Most of it you probably already have

108
00:03:55,985 --> 00:03:57,961
in your pantry right now,

109
00:03:57,961 --> 00:03:58,435
honey.

110
00:03:58,615 --> 00:04:00,542
That's the whole beauty of it.

111
00:04:00,892 --> 00:04:02,679
About the macaroni,

112
00:04:02,679 --> 00:04:03,713
sweetheart.

113
00:04:03,893 --> 00:04:06,592
Use plain elbow macaroni,

114
00:04:06,592 --> 00:04:08,212
the cheap kind.

115
00:04:08,392 --> 00:04:10,162
Don't use fancy pasta,

116
00:04:10,162 --> 00:04:12,736
and don't use the little shells,

117
00:04:12,736 --> 00:04:15,632
because they trap too much sauce and

118
00:04:15,632 --> 00:04:16,275
go soft.

119
00:04:16,455 --> 00:04:19,540
And don't cook the macaroni separately in

120
00:04:19,540 --> 00:04:20,669
a pot of water,

121
00:04:20,669 --> 00:04:22,550
the way some recipes say.

122
00:04:22,730 --> 00:04:24,226
That's the lazy way,

123
00:04:24,226 --> 00:04:26,469
and it makes a watery goulash,

124
00:04:26,469 --> 00:04:29,236
because the pasta never drinks up any

125
00:04:29,236 --> 00:04:30,881
of that tomato flavor.

126
00:04:31,061 --> 00:04:32,732
One pot, honey.

127
00:04:32,912 --> 00:04:34,712
Always one pot.

128
00:04:35,062 --> 00:04:37,664
Mistake number one is gray,

129
00:04:37,664 --> 00:04:38,916
steamed beef.

130
00:04:39,096 --> 00:04:41,712
People dump the meat in a cold

131
00:04:41,712 --> 00:04:43,194
pot, all at once,

132
00:04:43,194 --> 00:04:45,199
and stir it constantly,

133
00:04:45,199 --> 00:04:47,814
and it turns gray and boils in

134
00:04:47,814 --> 00:04:49,034
its own juice.

135
00:04:49,214 --> 00:04:50,526
No flavor.

136
00:04:50,706 --> 00:04:53,333
Get your big pot good and hot

137
00:04:53,333 --> 00:04:53,876
first.

138
00:04:54,056 --> 00:04:55,302
Put the beef in,

139
00:04:55,302 --> 00:04:56,393
press it flat,

140
00:04:56,393 --> 00:04:58,730
and leave it alone for four or

141
00:04:58,730 --> 00:05:01,613
five minutes, until the bottom gets a

142
00:05:01,613 --> 00:05:02,938
dark brown crust.

143
00:05:03,118 --> 00:05:04,395
Then break it up.

144
00:05:04,575 --> 00:05:06,966
That brown is where the flavor is.

145
00:05:07,316 --> 00:05:10,233
If Grandma's recipe is helping you,

146
00:05:10,233 --> 00:05:12,483
tap the like button for me,

147
00:05:12,483 --> 00:05:13,399
sweetheart.

148
00:05:13,579 --> 00:05:16,795
It helps more people find these videos.

149
00:05:16,975 --> 00:05:19,864
Mistake number two is not draining the

150
00:05:19,864 --> 00:05:20,168
fat.

151
00:05:20,348 --> 00:05:22,602
Goulash should be rich,

152
00:05:22,602 --> 00:05:23,680
not greasy.

153
00:05:23,860 --> 00:05:25,989
Once the beef is browned,

154
00:05:25,989 --> 00:05:28,202
spoon off most of the fat,

155
00:05:28,202 --> 00:05:30,501
leaving about a tablespoon.

156
00:05:30,681 --> 00:05:33,499
Then add your onion and green pepper,

157
00:05:33,499 --> 00:05:36,393
and cook them five minutes until soft.

158
00:05:36,573 --> 00:05:39,049
Add the garlic for the last minute,

159
00:05:39,049 --> 00:05:40,393
so it doesn't burn.

160
00:05:40,743 --> 00:05:43,677
Mistake number three is forgetting to cook

161
00:05:43,677 --> 00:05:44,864
the tomato paste.

162
00:05:45,044 --> 00:05:47,686
Most people stir it straight into the

163
00:05:47,686 --> 00:05:50,257
liquid, and it tastes raw and tinny.

164
00:05:50,437 --> 00:05:53,103
Push the beef and vegetables to the

165
00:05:53,103 --> 00:05:55,768
side, put the tomato paste right on

166
00:05:55,768 --> 00:05:57,748
the hot bottom of the pot,

167
00:05:57,748 --> 00:06:00,033
and let it sizzle for a minute

168
00:06:00,033 --> 00:06:02,470
or two, until it turns a darker,

169
00:06:02,470 --> 00:06:03,231
brick red.

170
00:06:03,411 --> 00:06:05,624
Then add the paprika and stir it

171
00:06:05,624 --> 00:06:07,974
into the paste for thirty seconds.

172
00:06:08,154 --> 00:06:10,174
That wakes the paprika up.

173
00:06:10,524 --> 00:06:12,943
Now add the crushed tomatoes,

174
00:06:12,943 --> 00:06:14,361
the tomato sauce,

175
00:06:14,361 --> 00:06:16,863
the broth, the Worcestershire,

176
00:06:16,863 --> 00:06:18,698
the Italian seasoning,

177
00:06:18,698 --> 00:06:19,950
the bay leaves,

178
00:06:19,950 --> 00:06:21,368
salt, and pepper.

179
00:06:21,548 --> 00:06:22,994
Stir it all together,

180
00:06:22,994 --> 00:06:25,474
scraping up every brown bit from the

181
00:06:25,474 --> 00:06:26,714
bottom of the pot.

182
00:06:26,894 --> 00:06:28,475
Bring it to a simmer,

183
00:06:28,475 --> 00:06:29,454
put a lid on,

184
00:06:29,454 --> 00:06:32,015
and let it cook gently for fifteen

185
00:06:32,015 --> 00:06:35,102
minutes before the macaroni ever goes in.

186
00:06:35,282 --> 00:06:37,451
In a minute I'll tell you why

187
00:06:37,451 --> 00:06:39,021
that waiting matters.

188
00:06:39,371 --> 00:06:41,145
So far, browned beef,

189
00:06:41,145 --> 00:06:44,524
no grease, and tomato paste cooked until

190
00:06:44,524 --> 00:06:45,791
it's brick red.

191
00:06:45,971 --> 00:06:49,066
Mistake number four is adding the macaroni

192
00:06:49,066 --> 00:06:49,802
too early.

193
00:06:49,982 --> 00:06:52,921
If the macaroni cooks the whole time,

194
00:06:52,921 --> 00:06:54,986
it turns soft and swollen.

195
00:06:55,166 --> 00:06:57,892
The sauce needs those fifteen minutes on

196
00:06:57,892 --> 00:06:59,868
its own to get rich and deep.

197
00:07:00,048 --> 00:07:02,283
Only then do you stir in the

198
00:07:02,283 --> 00:07:05,075
dry macaroni, right into the sauce.

199
00:07:05,255 --> 00:07:07,616
It cooks in the sauce and soaks

200
00:07:07,616 --> 00:07:09,064
up all that flavor.

201
00:07:09,414 --> 00:07:12,553
Mistake number five is cooking the macaroni

202
00:07:12,553 --> 00:07:13,721
until it's soft.

203
00:07:13,901 --> 00:07:14,966
Cover the pot,

204
00:07:14,966 --> 00:07:17,020
keep it at a gentle simmer,

205
00:07:17,020 --> 00:07:19,454
and stir every few minutes so it

206
00:07:19,454 --> 00:07:20,519
doesn't stick.

207
00:07:20,699 --> 00:07:23,276
Start tasting at about eight minutes.

208
00:07:23,456 --> 00:07:25,370
Take it off the heat when the

209
00:07:25,370 --> 00:07:27,679
macaroni still has a little bite in

210
00:07:27,679 --> 00:07:28,866
the middle, honey.

211
00:07:29,046 --> 00:07:31,612
It keeps cooking in the hot sauce.

212
00:07:31,792 --> 00:07:33,914
If you wait until it's soft in

213
00:07:33,914 --> 00:07:35,965
the pot, it'll be mush on the

214
00:07:35,965 --> 00:07:36,390
plate.

215
00:07:36,740 --> 00:07:38,592
If it looks too thick,

216
00:07:38,592 --> 00:07:40,443
add a splash of broth.

217
00:07:40,623 --> 00:07:42,185
If it looks too thin,

218
00:07:42,185 --> 00:07:44,193
leave the lid off for a few

219
00:07:44,193 --> 00:07:46,870
minutes, and it'll tighten right up.

220
00:07:47,050 --> 00:07:48,884
Pull out the bay leaves.

221
00:07:49,064 --> 00:07:51,105
And let it sit for five minutes

222
00:07:51,105 --> 00:07:53,278
off the heat before you serve it.

223
00:07:53,458 --> 00:07:54,453
Stay with me,

224
00:07:54,453 --> 00:07:57,975
because now comes my mother's Thursday secret.

225
00:07:58,325 --> 00:08:00,496
Now, about the cheese.

226
00:08:00,676 --> 00:08:03,470
Don't stir the cheddar into the whole

227
00:08:03,470 --> 00:08:06,567
pot, honey, or the leftovers turn stringy

228
00:08:06,567 --> 00:08:07,398
and greasy.

229
00:08:07,578 --> 00:08:09,086
Put it on each bowl,

230
00:08:09,086 --> 00:08:11,047
right before you serve it,

231
00:08:11,047 --> 00:08:13,082
so it melts on top from the

232
00:08:13,082 --> 00:08:14,590
heat of the goulash.

233
00:08:14,770 --> 00:08:17,436
And use real sharp cheddar that you

234
00:08:17,436 --> 00:08:18,579
shred yourself.

235
00:08:18,759 --> 00:08:21,332
The bagged kind is coated in powder

236
00:08:21,332 --> 00:08:22,656
so it won't clump,

237
00:08:22,656 --> 00:08:24,494
and it never melts right.

238
00:08:24,844 --> 00:08:26,777
Here's my mother's secret,

239
00:08:26,777 --> 00:08:27,595
sweetheart.

240
00:08:27,775 --> 00:08:28,986
At the very end,

241
00:08:28,986 --> 00:08:31,255
after the bay leaves came out,

242
00:08:31,255 --> 00:08:33,979
she stirred in one teaspoon of sugar

243
00:08:33,979 --> 00:08:37,005
and one teaspoon of apple cider vinegar.

244
00:08:37,185 --> 00:08:38,149
That's all.

245
00:08:38,329 --> 00:08:40,518
The sugar takes away the tinny,

246
00:08:40,518 --> 00:08:42,567
sour edge of canned tomatoes.

247
00:08:42,747 --> 00:08:45,223
The vinegar wakes everything up,

248
00:08:45,223 --> 00:08:47,158
so it doesn't taste flat.

249
00:08:47,338 --> 00:08:48,975
You won't taste either one.

250
00:08:49,155 --> 00:08:51,424
But you'll taste the difference,

251
00:08:51,424 --> 00:08:51,849
honey.

252
00:08:52,029 --> 00:08:55,431
It goes from cafeteria to Sunday dinner.

253
00:08:55,781 --> 00:08:57,754
She learned it from Mrs.

254
00:08:57,934 --> 00:08:59,389
Kowalski next door,

255
00:08:59,389 --> 00:09:01,838
the mother of those two boys who

256
00:09:01,838 --> 00:09:03,751
showed up every Thursday.

257
00:09:03,931 --> 00:09:05,981
She was from the old country,

258
00:09:05,981 --> 00:09:08,455
and she said every good tomato dish

259
00:09:08,455 --> 00:09:10,787
needs a little sweet and a little

260
00:09:10,787 --> 00:09:11,141
sour.

261
00:09:11,321 --> 00:09:13,073
So it was a trade,

262
00:09:13,073 --> 00:09:13,852
you see.

263
00:09:14,032 --> 00:09:14,879
Mrs.

264
00:09:15,059 --> 00:09:17,859
Kowalski gave my mother the secret,

265
00:09:17,859 --> 00:09:20,418
and my mother fed her boys every

266
00:09:20,418 --> 00:09:22,258
Thursday for ten years.

267
00:09:22,438 --> 00:09:24,342
Nobody ever mentioned it.

268
00:09:24,522 --> 00:09:27,354
That's how neighbors worked back then.

269
00:09:27,705 --> 00:09:30,001
Serve it in bowls with a handful

270
00:09:30,001 --> 00:09:32,226
of sharp cheddar melted on top,

271
00:09:32,226 --> 00:09:34,522
and a slice of buttered bread on

272
00:09:34,522 --> 00:09:36,819
the side to wipe the bowl clean.

273
00:09:36,999 --> 00:09:39,334
Some families add a can of corn

274
00:09:39,334 --> 00:09:40,540
or kidney beans.

275
00:09:40,720 --> 00:09:43,402
Some add a pinch of chili powder.

276
00:09:43,582 --> 00:09:45,775
Harold liked his with a shake of

277
00:09:45,775 --> 00:09:46,461
hot sauce.

278
00:09:46,641 --> 00:09:49,750
Leftovers are even better the next day,

279
00:09:49,750 --> 00:09:50,228
honey.

280
00:09:50,408 --> 00:09:52,540
Add a splash of water when you

281
00:09:52,540 --> 00:09:55,454
reheat it, because the macaroni drinks up

282
00:09:55,454 --> 00:09:56,875
the sauce overnight.

283
00:09:57,225 --> 00:09:59,485
The Kowalski boys grew up,

284
00:09:59,485 --> 00:10:00,441
sweetheart.

285
00:10:00,621 --> 00:10:03,146
One of them became a pharmacist in

286
00:10:03,146 --> 00:10:05,151
town, and when I went in to

287
00:10:05,151 --> 00:10:07,082
pick up Harold's medicine,

288
00:10:07,082 --> 00:10:08,642
the year he was sick,

289
00:10:08,642 --> 00:10:10,350
he wouldn't let me pay.

290
00:10:10,530 --> 00:10:11,878
I said, Stanley,

291
00:10:11,878 --> 00:10:13,479
what are you doing?

292
00:10:13,659 --> 00:10:15,633
He said, Mrs.

293
00:10:15,813 --> 00:10:18,648
Ruth, your mother fed me goulash every

294
00:10:18,648 --> 00:10:20,364
Thursday for ten years.

295
00:10:20,544 --> 00:10:22,332
I'm just catching up.

296
00:10:22,512 --> 00:10:25,066
I cried right there at the counter.

297
00:10:25,416 --> 00:10:26,827
Make it this week,

298
00:10:26,827 --> 00:10:29,807
sweetheart, and set out an extra bowl,

299
00:10:29,807 --> 00:10:30,826
just in case.

300
00:10:31,006 --> 00:10:32,813
Tell me in the comments,

301
00:10:32,813 --> 00:10:35,522
what did your family call this dish?

302
00:10:35,702 --> 00:10:37,586
Goulash, chili mac,

303
00:10:37,586 --> 00:10:39,371
or something else?

304
00:10:39,551 --> 00:10:41,965
There are more of my everyday recipes

305
00:10:41,965 --> 00:10:44,249
in the playlist right there on your

306
00:10:44,249 --> 00:10:44,706
screen.

307
00:10:44,886 --> 00:10:46,616
Good night, honey.
